{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"## 🟡 §5 — When You Don't Want a Single Address\n\nTwo deliberate exceptions. They only make sense now — a reader meeting either of these before §3 and §4 has no idea"}},"session_id":"cb2ef54b-7da5-4ab3-bcf1-341d245d917b","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"0697e80e-a064-4ad0-8b31-eff9c1e82a03"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" what is being subtracted from what.\n\n### Headless: no single address, on purpose\n\nSometimes you don't need load-balancing and a single Service IP. In that case, you can create what are termed **headless Services**, by explicitly specifying `\"None\"` for the cluster IP address (`.spec.clusterI"}},"session_id":"cb2ef54b-7da5-4ab3-bcf1-341d245d917b","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"abbfc2e2-86ba-4f46-81bc-20f72253d126"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"P`) [source: k8s-docs-service-2026-08-23].\n\nWhat changes depends on whether the Service has a selector:\n\n- For headless Services that **define selectors**, the endpoints controller creates EndpointSlices in the Kubernetes API, and modifies the DNS configuration to return **A or"}},"session_id":"cb2ef54b-7da5-4ab3-bcf1-341d245d917b","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"5e94a85e-7a2d-4da5-9f12-2d5e78a286c7"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" AAAA records that point directly to the Pods** backing the Service [source: k8s-docs-service-2026-08-23].\n- For headless Services **without selectors**, no EndpointSlices are created automatically [source: k8s-docs-service-2026-08-23].\n\nThat first bullet carries the K"}},"session_id":"cb2ef54b-7da5-4ab3-bcf1-341d245d917b","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"35568c5f-4ac5-4001-b64b-43c7977ec171"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ubernetes documentation's own phrasing — *the endpoints controller*. §4 called the same component **the EndpointSlice controller**. These are two names for one job, not two controllers: whichever name you meet, the object being written is an EndpointSlice.\n\n★ **Fixed Point:** `"}},"session_id":"cb2ef54b-7da5-4ab3-bcf1-341d245d917b","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"75fd3316-7c80-4615-b7f3-b7185a984bfd"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"clusterIP: None` is a **configuration, not a failure**. It says: *do not give me one address — give me all of them.* DNS returns the Pod addresses directly instead of a single virtual IP.\n\n> 🪝 **Snag:** \"Headless\" sounds like something went wrong, and a teamm"}},"session_id":"cb2ef54b-7da5-4ab3-bcf1-341d245d917b","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"3da1c73d-7432-4a1f-8d4a-a2d2e350eb8e"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ate seeing `<none>` in the CLUSTER-IP column will often say so. It means the Service has no *head* — no single virtual IP standing in front of the set, no flagship to hail on the whole fleet's behalf. The Pods are all still there. You just get all of them, instead of one of them.\n\n###"}},"session_id":"cb2ef54b-7da5-4ab3-bcf1-341d245d917b","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"c09962cf-c879-4b8e-bcf3-fc7230b2b083"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" Why anyone would want that\n\nChapter 6 already handed you the answer and then deferred it.\n\n**StatefulSets currently require a headless Service to be responsible for the network identity of the Pods. You are responsible for creating this Service** [source: k8s-docs-statef"}},"session_id":"cb2ef54b-7da5-4ab3-bcf1-341d245d917b","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"4d0f693d-9d00-4289-9a86-a04c441d9390"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ulset-2026-08-24].\n\nThink about what a StatefulSet is for. Its Pods are created from the same spec, but **are not interchangeable**: each has a persistent identifier that it maintains across any rescheduling [source:"}},"session_id":"cb2ef54b-7da5-4ab3-bcf1-341d245d917b","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"e9cfafea-0cf4-4dcb-b8e0-fea2939b3fbf"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" k8s-docs-statefulset-2026-08-24]. A database with one primary and two replicas. A quorum of peers that need to know each other by name. In those workloads, \"send this to any one of them, I don't care which\" is not a convenience — it is *precisely wrong*. If you"}},"session_id":"cb2ef54b-7da5-4ab3-bcf1-341d245d917b","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"11d73c0b-d53f-421b-b3e6-617f4dbab4da"}
